# BTOON — Binary TOON · Executable Spec Walkthrough

## Section

An executable companion to the [BTOON specification](btoon.md). BTOON is a
compact, deterministic, high-performance **binary encoding of the TOON data
model** — the relationship BSON has to JSON. The TOON text side of the model is
implemented by the [`toon_ex`](https://hex.pm/packages/toon_ex) Elixir package
([docs](https://toon-ex.hexdocs.pm/)); this notebook implements the BTOON
binary side from scratch, in plain Elixir, with **zero dependencies**.

You will:

* build a minimal spec-conformant codec — envelope, SmallInt, string table,
  TypedArray, ObjectTable, schema mode, alignment padding;
* verify it byte-for-byte against every test vector in
  [§26](btoon.md#26-test-vectors) of the spec;
* watch alignment padding and determinism rules do their job on real bytes.

## 1. A minimal spec-conformant codec

One cell, one module. It implements the spec's cold and hot paths:

| Spec section | What the codec does                                                   |
| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| §7          | envelope: magic `"BTON"`, version, flags, reserved                    |
| §8–§9    | SmallInt (−32..95), Int32/Int64, Float32/Float64, strings, binaries |
| §10–§11  | arrays, objects (sorted keys), per-message string table               |
| §13, §16   | TypedArray with element-size alignment padding                        |
| §14         | ObjectTable (column mode)                                             |
| §7.6, §15  | schema mode with embedded authoritative schema, padded block          |
| §19         | trailing-byte rejection, flag/body consistency                        |

Out of scope for the walkthrough: session dictionary (flags 0x08/0x10),
extension types (§23), change tracking (§20).

```elixir
defmodule BTOON.Example do
  @moduledoc """
  Minimal BTOON (Binary TOON) codec for the Livebook walkthrough.

  Implements the spec end to end:

  * envelope: magic "BTON", version 1, flags, reserved (§7)
  * per-message string table, flag 0x04, first-encounter order, 8-byte pad (§7.5, §11)
  * SmallInt −32..95, Int32/Int64, Float32/Float64 (§8, §9)
  * StringRef, arrays, objects with sorted keys (§9, §10, §17)
  * TypedArray tag 0x0C with element-size alignment padding (§13, §16)
  * ObjectTable tag 0x0D, columnar numeric tables (§14)
  * schema mode, flag 0x02, embedded authoritative schema, padded block (§7.6, §15)
  * schema ID as UInt16, flag 0x20 (§7.6.1)
  * trailing-byte rejection and flag/body consistency (§19)

  Out of scope for the walkthrough: session dictionary (flags 0x08/0x10),
  extension types (§23), change tracking (§20).
  """

  @magic "BTON"

  defmodule Raw do
    @enforce_keys [:bytes]
    defstruct [:bytes]
  end

  defmodule TypedArray do
    @enforce_keys [:element_type, :values]
    defstruct [:element_type, :values]
  end

  defmodule ObjectTable do
    @enforce_keys [:columns]
    defstruct [:columns]
  end

  @elem_types %{
    int8: {0x00, 1}, uint8: {0x01, 1},
    int16: {0x02, 2}, uint16: {0x03, 2},
    int32: {0x04, 4}, uint32: {0x05, 4},
    int64: {0x06, 8}, uint64: {0x0F, 8},
    float32: {0x07, 4}, float64: {0x08, 8},
    null: {0x09, 0}, bool: {0x0A, 1},
    string: {0x0B, 0}, binary: {0x0C, 0},
    array: {0x0D, 0}, object: {0x0E, 0}
  }

  @int_order [:int8, :uint8, :int16, :uint16, :int32, :uint32, :int64, :uint64]
  @sel_to_type Map.new(@elem_types, fn {t, {sel, _}} -> {sel, t} end)

  import Bitwise

  ## ── Encode ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  def encode(data, opts \\ []) do
    schema = Keyword.get(opts, :schema)
    id16? = Keyword.get(opts, :schema_id_uint16, false)
    table_mode = Keyword.get(opts, :string_table, :auto) == :auto

    strings =
      if schema do
        Enum.flat_map(schema.fields, fn {name, _t} -> collect(Map.fetch!(data, name)) end)
      else
        collect(data)
      end
      |> Enum.uniq()

    table? = table_mode and strings != []
    refs = if table?, do: Map.new(Enum.with_index(strings)), else: %{}

    flags =
      0
      |> maybe_flag(table?, 0x04)
      |> maybe_flag(schema != nil, 0x02)
      |> maybe_flag(id16? and schema != nil, 0x20)

    {table_iodata, table_size} =
      if table? do
        content =
          IO.iodata_to_binary([
            <<length(strings)::32-little>>,
            Enum.map(strings, &[<<byte_size(&1)::32-little>>, &1])
          ])

        pad_to_8(content)
      else
        {[], 0}
      end

    {schema_iodata, schema_size} =
      if schema, do: pad_to_8(IO.iodata_to_binary(schema_block(schema, id16?))), else: {[], 0}

    base = 8 + table_size + schema_size

    {body, _off} =
      if schema, do: enc_schema_body(data, schema, refs, id16?, base), else: enc(data, refs, opts, base)

    IO.iodata_to_binary([@magic, <<1>>, <<flags>>, <<0, 0>>, table_iodata, schema_iodata, body])
  end

  defp maybe_flag(acc, true, bit), do: acc + bit
  defp maybe_flag(acc, false, _bit), do: acc

  defp pad_to_8(content) do
    pad = rem(8 - rem(byte_size(content), 8), 8)
    {[content, :binary.copy(<<0>>, pad)], byte_size(content) + pad}
  end

  defp enc(nil, _refs, _opts, off), do: {<<0x00>>, off + 1}
  defp enc(false, _refs, _opts, off), do: {<<0x01>>, off + 1}
  defp enc(true, _refs, _opts, off), do: {<<0x02>>, off + 1}

  defp enc(n, _refs, _opts, off) when is_integer(n) and n in -32..95, do: {<<n + 64>>, off + 1}

  defp enc(n, _refs, _opts, off) when is_integer(n) and n in -2_147_483_648..2_147_483_647,
    do: {<<0x03, n::signed-little-size(32)>>, off + 5}

  defp enc(n, _refs, _opts, off) when is_integer(n), do: {<<0x04, n::signed-little-size(64)>>, off + 9}

  defp enc(f, _refs, _opts, off) when is_float(f) do
    if float32_lossless?(f) do
      {<<0x05, f::float-little-size(32)>>, off + 5}
    else
      {<<0x06, f::float-little-size(64)>>, off + 9}
    end
  end

  defp enc(%Raw{bytes: b}, _refs, _opts, off),
    do: {<<0x08, byte_size(b)::32-little, b::binary>>, off + 5 + byte_size(b)}

  defp enc(s, refs, _opts, off) when is_binary(s) do
    case refs do
      %{^s => id} -> {<<0x0B, int_bytes(id)::binary>>, off + 1 + int_size(id)}
      _ -> {<<0x07, byte_size(s)::32-little, s::binary>>, off + 5 + byte_size(s)}
    end
  end

  defp enc(%TypedArray{element_type: t, values: vs}, _refs, _opts, off) do
    {sel, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
    buf = IO.iodata_to_binary(Enum.map(vs, &enc_elem(&1, t)))
    pad = align_pad(off + 7, sz)
    {[<<0x0C, sel, length(vs)::32-little, pad>>, :binary.copy(<<0>>, pad), buf], off + 7 + pad + byte_size(buf)}
  end

  defp enc(%ObjectTable{columns: cols}, refs, opts, off) do
    cols = Enum.sort_by(cols, &elem(&1, 0))
    rowcount = cols |> hd() |> elem(2) |> length()

    {cols_iodata, _off2} =
      Enum.map_reduce(cols, off + 9, fn {name, t, vs}, o ->
        {name_iodata, o1} = enc(name, refs, opts, o)
        {sel, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
        buf = IO.iodata_to_binary(Enum.map(vs, &enc_elem(&1, t)))
        pad = align_pad(o1 + 2, sz)
        {[name_iodata, <<sel, pad>>, :binary.copy(<<0>>, pad), buf], o1 + 2 + pad + byte_size(buf)}
      end)

    {[<<0x0D, rowcount::32-little, length(cols)::32-little>>, cols_iodata],
     off + 9 + IO.iodata_length(cols_iodata)}
  end

  defp enc(list, refs, opts, off) when is_list(list) do
    if Keyword.get(opts, :typed_arrays, true) and homogeneous_numeric?(list) do
      t = pick_elem_type(list)
      {sel, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
      buf = IO.iodata_to_binary(Enum.map(list, &enc_elem(&1, t)))
      pad = align_pad(off + 7, sz)
      {[<<0x0C, sel, length(list)::32-little, pad>>, :binary.copy(<<0>>, pad), buf], off + 7 + pad + byte_size(buf)}
    else
      {items, off2} = Enum.map_reduce(list, off + 5, &enc(&1, refs, opts, &2))
      {[<<0x09, length(list)::32-little>>, items], off2}
    end
  end

  defp enc(map, refs, opts, off) when is_map(map) do
    {pairs, off2} =
      map
      |> Enum.sort()
      |> Enum.map_reduce(off + 5, fn {k, v}, o ->
        {ke, o1} = enc(k, refs, opts, o)
        {ve, o2} = enc(v, refs, opts, o1)
        {[ke, ve], o2}
      end)

    {[<<0x0A, map_size(map)::32-little>>, pairs], off2}
  end

  defp enc_schema_body(data, schema, refs, id16?, base) do
    id = if id16?, do: <<schema.id::size(16)-little>>, else: <<schema.id::size(32)-little>>

    {vals, off} =
      Enum.map_reduce(schema.fields, base + byte_size(id), fn {name, t}, o ->
        enc_field(Map.fetch!(data, name), t, refs, o)
      end)

    {[id, vals], off}
  end

  defp enc_field(v, t, _refs, off) when t in [:int8, :int16, :int32, :int64] do
    {_, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
    {<<v::signed-little-size(sz * 8)>>, off + sz}
  end

  defp enc_field(v, t, _refs, off) when t in [:uint8, :uint16, :uint32, :uint64] do
    {_, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
    {<<v::unsigned-little-size(sz * 8)>>, off + sz}
  end

  defp enc_field(v, :float32, _refs, off), do: {<<v::float-little-size(32)>>, off + 4}
  defp enc_field(v, :float64, _refs, off), do: {<<v::float-little-size(64)>>, off + 8}
  defp enc_field(nil, :null, _refs, off), do: {"", off}
  defp enc_field(v, :bool, _refs, off), do: {<<(if v, do: 1, else: 0)>>, off + 1}

  defp enc_field(v, :binary, refs, off) when is_binary(v), do: enc(%Raw{bytes: v}, refs, [], off)
  defp enc_field(v, _t, refs, off), do: enc(v, refs, [], off)

  defp schema_block(schema, id16?) do
    id = if id16?, do: <<schema.id::size(16)-little>>, else: <<schema.id::size(32)-little>>

    fields =
      Enum.map(schema.fields, fn {name, t} ->
        {sel, _sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
        [<<byte_size(name)::32-little>>, name, <<sel>>]
      end)

    [id, <<byte_size(schema.name)::32-little>>, schema.name, <<length(schema.fields)::32-little>>, fields]
  end

  defp collect(nil), do: []
  defp collect(v) when is_integer(v) or is_float(v) or is_boolean(v), do: []
  defp collect(%Raw{}), do: []
  defp collect(%TypedArray{}), do: []

  defp collect(%ObjectTable{columns: cols}),
    do: Enum.flat_map(cols, fn {name, _t, _vs} -> collect(name) end)

  defp collect(s) when is_binary(s), do: [s]
  defp collect(list) when is_list(list), do: Enum.flat_map(list, &collect/1)

  defp collect(map) when is_map(map) do
    map
    |> Enum.sort()
    |> Enum.flat_map(fn {k, v} -> collect(k) ++ collect(v) end)
  end

  ## ── Encode helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  defp homogeneous_numeric?(list),
    do: list != [] and (Enum.all?(list, &is_integer/1) or Enum.all?(list, &is_float/1))

  defp pick_elem_type(values) do
    if Enum.all?(values, &is_float/1) do
      if Enum.all?(values, &float32_lossless?/1), do: :float32, else: :float64
    else
      min = Enum.min(values)
      max = Enum.max(values)
      Enum.find(@int_order, &fits?(min, max, &1))
    end
  end

  # Signed-first, matching the §26.4 vector: when a signed and an unsigned type
  # of the same width both fit, the signed type is used.
  defp fits?(min, max, :int8), do: min >= -128 and max <= 127
  defp fits?(min, max, :uint8), do: min >= 0 and max <= 255
  defp fits?(min, max, :int16), do: min >= -32_768 and max <= 32_767
  defp fits?(min, max, :uint16), do: min >= 0 and max <= 65_535
  defp fits?(min, max, :int32), do: min >= -2_147_483_648 and max <= 2_147_483_647
  defp fits?(min, max, :uint32), do: min >= 0 and max <= 4_294_967_295
  defp fits?(_min, _max, :int64), do: true
  defp fits?(min, _max, :uint64), do: min >= 0

  defp enc_elem(v, t) do
    {_, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)

    cond do
      t in [:float32, :float64] -> <<v::float-little-size(sz * 8)>>
      t in [:int8, :int16, :int32, :int64] -> <<v::signed-little-size(sz * 8)>>
      true -> <<v::unsigned-little-size(sz * 8)>>
    end
  end

  defp float32_lossless?(f) do
    <<g::float-little-size(32)>> = <<f::float-little-size(32)>>
    g == f
  end

  defp align_pad(target_off, elem_size), do: rem(elem_size - rem(target_off, elem_size), elem_size)

  defp int_bytes(id) when id in -32..95, do: <<id + 64>>
  defp int_bytes(id) when id in -2_147_483_648..2_147_483_647, do: <<id::signed-little-size(32)>>
  defp int_bytes(id), do: <<id::signed-little-size(64)>>

  defp int_size(id) when id in -32..95, do: 1
  defp int_size(id) when id in -2_147_483_648..2_147_483_647, do: 4
  defp int_size(_id), do: 8

  ## ── Decode ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  def decode(bin, _opts \\ []) do
    do_decode(bin)
  rescue
    e in [MatchError, ArgumentError, FunctionClauseError] -> {:error, Exception.message(e)}
  end

  def decode!(bin, opts \\ []) do
    {:ok, value} = decode(bin, opts)
    value
  end

  defp do_decode(<<@magic, 1, flags, 0, 0, rest::binary>>) do
    {strings, off, rest} = parse_table(rest, flags, 8)
    refs = strings |> Enum.with_index() |> Map.new(fn {s, i} -> {i, s} end)
    {schema, _id16?, off, rest} = parse_schema_block(rest, flags, off)

    {value, _off, rest} =
      if (flags &&& 0x02) != 0 do
        decode_schema_body(rest, flags, off, schema, refs)
      else
        parse_value(rest, off, refs)
      end

    case rest do
      <<>> -> {:ok, value}
      _ -> {:error, :trailing_bytes}
    end
  end

  defp do_decode(_), do: {:error, :bad_header}

  defp parse_table(rest, flags, off) do
    if (flags &&& 0x04) != 0 do
      {count, rest} = u32(rest)
      {strings, consumed, rest} = parse_strings(rest, count, 4, [])
      pad = pad_len(consumed)
      {_pad_bytes, rest} = skip_pad(rest, pad)
      {strings, off + consumed + pad, rest}
    else
      {[], off, rest}
    end
  end

  defp parse_strings(rest, 0, consumed, acc), do: {:lists.reverse(acc), consumed, rest}

  defp parse_strings(rest, n, consumed, acc) do
    {len, rest} = u32(rest)
    <<s::size(^len)-binary, rest::binary>> = rest
    parse_strings(rest, n - 1, consumed + 4 + len, [s | acc])
  end

  defp parse_schema_block(rest, flags, off) do
    if (flags &&& 0x02) != 0 do
      id16? = (flags &&& 0x20) != 0
      idsz = if id16?, do: 2, else: 4
      {id, rest} = if id16?, do: u16(rest), else: u32(rest)
      {name_len, rest} = u32(rest)
      <<name::size(^name_len)-binary, rest::binary>> = rest
      {nfields, rest} = u32(rest)
      {fields, fields_sz, rest} = parse_fields(rest, nfields, 0, [])
      consumed = idsz + 4 + name_len + 4 + fields_sz
      pad = pad_len(consumed)
      {_pad_bytes, rest} = skip_pad(rest, pad)
      schema = %{id: id, name: name, fields: fields}
      {schema, id16?, off + consumed + pad, rest}
    else
      {nil, false, off, rest}
    end
  end

  defp parse_fields(rest, 0, sz, acc), do: {Enum.reverse(acc), sz, rest}

  defp parse_fields(rest, n, sz, acc) do
    {len, rest} = u32(rest)
    <<fname::size(^len)-binary, sel, rest::binary>> = rest
    parse_fields(rest, n - 1, sz + 4 + len + 1, [{fname, sel_to_type!(sel)} | acc])
  end

  defp decode_schema_body(rest, flags, off, schema, refs) do
    id16? = (flags &&& 0x20) != 0
    idsz = if id16?, do: 2, else: 4
    {body_id, rest} = if id16?, do: u16(rest), else: u32(rest)
    true = body_id == schema.id
    decode_fields(rest, off + idsz, refs, schema.fields, %{})
  end

  defp decode_fields(rest, off, _refs, [], acc), do: {acc, off, rest}

  defp decode_fields(rest, off, refs, [{name, t} | tf], acc) do
    {v, off, rest} = decode_field(rest, off, refs, t)
    decode_fields(rest, off, refs, tf, Map.put(acc, name, v))
  end

  defp decode_field(rest, off, _refs, t) when t in [:int8, :int16, :int32, :int64] do
    {_, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
    <<v::signed-little-size(^sz * 8), rest::binary>> = rest
    {v, off + sz, rest}
  end

  defp decode_field(rest, off, _refs, t) when t in [:uint8, :uint16, :uint32, :uint64] do
    {_, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
    <<v::unsigned-little-size(^sz * 8), rest::binary>> = rest
    {v, off + sz, rest}
  end

  defp decode_field(<<v::float-little-size(32), rest::binary>>, off, _refs, :float32),
    do: {v, off + 4, rest}

  defp decode_field(<<v::float-little-size(64), rest::binary>>, off, _refs, :float64),
    do: {v, off + 8, rest}

  defp decode_field(rest, off, _refs, :null), do: {nil, off, rest}
  defp decode_field(<<x, rest::binary>>, off, _refs, :bool), do: {x == 1, off + 1, rest}

  defp decode_field(rest, off, _refs, :binary) do
    {%Raw{bytes: b}, off, rest} = parse_value(rest, off, %{})
    {b, off, rest}
  end

  defp decode_field(rest, off, refs, t) when t in [:string, :array, :object],
    do: parse_value(rest, off, refs)

  defp parse_value(<<tag, rest::binary>>, off, refs) do
    case tag do
      0x00 -> {nil, off + 1, rest}
      0x01 -> {false, off + 1, rest}
      0x02 -> {true, off + 1, rest}
      t when t in 0x20..0x9F -> {t - 64, off + 1, rest}

      0x03 ->
        <<n::signed-little-size(32), rest::binary>> = rest
        {n, off + 5, rest}

      0x04 ->
        <<n::signed-little-size(64), rest::binary>> = rest
        {n, off + 9, rest}

      0x05 ->
        <<v::float-little-size(32), rest::binary>> = rest
        {v, off + 5, rest}

      0x06 ->
        <<v::float-little-size(64), rest::binary>> = rest
        {v, off + 9, rest}

      0x07 ->
        {len, rest} = u32(rest)
        <<s::size(^len)-binary, rest::binary>> = rest
        {s, off + 5 + len, rest}

      0x08 ->
        {len, rest} = u32(rest)
        <<b::size(^len)-binary, rest::binary>> = rest
        {%Raw{bytes: b}, off + 5 + len, rest}

      0x09 ->
        {count, rest} = u32(rest)
        parse_items(rest, off + 5, refs, count, [])

      0x0A ->
        {count, rest} = u32(rest)
        {pairs, off2, rest2} = parse_pairs(rest, off + 5, refs, count, [])
        {Map.new(pairs), off2, rest2}

      0x0B ->
        {id, rest} = parse_int(rest)
        {Map.fetch!(refs, id), off + 1 + int_size(id), rest}

      0x0C ->
        <<sel, count::32-little, padlen, rest::binary>> = rest
        t = sel_to_type!(sel)
        true = t in [:int8, :uint8, :int16, :uint16, :int32, :uint32, :int64, :uint64, :float32, :float64]
        {_, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
        true = padlen == align_pad(off + 7, sz)
        {_pad_bytes, rest} = skip_pad(rest, padlen)
        buflen = count * sz
        <<buf::size(^buflen)-binary, rest::binary>> = rest

        {%TypedArray{element_type: t, values: decode_elems(buf, t, sz)},
         off + 7 + padlen + buflen, rest}

      0x0D ->
        {rows, rest} = u32(rest)
        {ncols, rest} = u32(rest)
        {cols, off2, rest2} = parse_columns(rest, off + 9, refs, ncols, rows, [])
        {%ObjectTable{columns: cols}, off2, rest2}

      tag ->
        raise ArgumentError, "unknown tag #{inspect(tag, base: :hex)} at offset #{off}"
    end
  end

  defp parse_items(rest, off, _refs, 0, acc), do: {:lists.reverse(acc), off, rest}

  defp parse_items(rest, off, refs, n, acc) do
    {v, off, rest} = parse_value(rest, off, refs)
    parse_items(rest, off, refs, n - 1, [v | acc])
  end

  defp parse_pairs(rest, off, _refs, 0, acc), do: {:lists.reverse(acc), off, rest}

  defp parse_pairs(rest, off, refs, n, acc) do
    {k, off, rest} = parse_value(rest, off, refs)
    {v, off, rest} = parse_value(rest, off, refs)
    parse_pairs(rest, off, refs, n - 1, [{k, v} | acc])
  end

  defp parse_columns(rest, off, _refs, 0, _rows, acc), do: {:lists.reverse(acc), off, rest}

  defp parse_columns(rest, off, refs, n, rows, acc) do
    {name, off, rest} = parse_value(rest, off, refs)
    <<sel, padlen, rest::binary>> = rest
    t = sel_to_type!(sel)
    {_, sz} = Map.fetch!(@elem_types, t)
    true = padlen == align_pad(off + 2, sz)
    {_pad_bytes, rest} = skip_pad(rest, padlen)
    buflen = rows * sz
    <<buf::size(^buflen)-binary, rest::binary>> = rest
    parse_columns(rest, off + 2 + padlen + buflen, refs, n - 1, rows, [{name, t, decode_elems(buf, t, sz)} | acc])
  end

  defp parse_int(<<b, rest::binary>>) when b in 0x20..0x9F, do: {b - 64, rest}
  defp parse_int(<<0x03, n::signed-little-size(32), rest::binary>>), do: {n, rest}
  defp parse_int(<<0x04, n::signed-little-size(64), rest::binary>>), do: {n, rest}

  defp decode_elems(buf, t, sz) do
    for <<chunk::size(^sz)-binary <- buf>> do
      cond do
        t in [:float32, :float64] ->
          <<v::float-little-size(^sz * 8)>> = chunk
          v

        t in [:int8, :int16, :int32, :int64] ->
          <<v::signed-little-size(^sz * 8)>> = chunk
          v

        true ->
          <<v::unsigned-little-size(^sz * 8)>> = chunk
          v
      end
    end
  end

  defp u16(<<n::size(16)-little, rest::binary>>), do: {n, rest}
  defp u32(<<n::size(32)-little, rest::binary>>), do: {n, rest}

  defp pad_len(consumed), do: rem(8 - rem(consumed, 8), 8)

  defp skip_pad(rest, 0), do: {"", rest}

  defp skip_pad(rest, padlen) do
    n = padlen
    <<pad::size(^n)-binary, rest2::binary>> = rest
    true = pad == :binary.copy(<<0>>, n)
    {pad, rest2}
  end

  defp sel_to_type!(sel) do
    case Map.fetch(@sel_to_type, sel) do
      {:ok, t} -> t
      :error -> raise ArgumentError, "invalid element type selector #{inspect(sel, base: :hex)}"
    end
  end

  ## ── Utilities ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  def to_rows(%ObjectTable{columns: []}), do: []

  def to_rows(%ObjectTable{columns: cols}) do
    names = Enum.map(cols, &elem(&1, 0))
    vals = Enum.map(cols, &elem(&1, 2))

    for i <- 0..(vals |> hd() |> length()) - 1 do
      names |> Enum.zip(Enum.map(vals, &Enum.at(&1, i))) |> Map.new()
    end
  end

  def hexdump(bin) when is_binary(bin) do
    bin
    |> :binary.bin_to_list()
    |> Enum.chunk_every(16, 16, [])
    |> Enum.with_index()
    |> Enum.map_join("\n", fn {chunk, i} ->
      hex =
        chunk
        |> Enum.map_join(" ", &:io_lib.format("~2.16.0B", [&1]) |> IO.iodata_to_binary())
        |> String.pad_trailing(16 * 3 - 1)

      ascii =
        chunk
        |> Enum.map(fn b -> if b in 32..126, do: <<b>>, else: "." end)
        |> Enum.join()

      :io_lib.format("~4.16.0B  ~s  ~s", [i * 16, hex, ascii]) |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
    end)
  end
end
```

## 2. Envelope, tags, and SmallInt (§7–§8)

Every message starts with the 8-byte header `42 54 4F 4E` ("BTON") + version +
flags + reserved. Every value starts with a one-byte tag — except integers in
−32..95, which are the tag *and* the value in a single byte:

```elixir
alias BTOON.Example.{Raw, TypedArray, ObjectTable}
import BTOON.Example

IO.puts("42  →  " <> hexdump(encode(42)))
IO.puts("")
IO.puts("96  →  " <> hexdump(encode(96)))
IO.puts("")
IO.puts("1.5 →  " <> hexdump(encode(1.5)))
```

* `42` encodes as bare `6A` (SmallInt: `42 + 64 = 0x6A`) — one read, one branch.
* `96` just misses SmallInt, so it becomes tag `03` + Int32.
* `1.5` survives a binary32 round-trip exactly, so the encoder MUST use Float32
  (determinism, §17).

## 3. Byte-for-byte spec vectors (§26)

The spec's test vectors are executable. Each assertion compares the encoded
bytes against the exact hex from §26.1–§26.6:

```elixir
vectors = [
  {nil,                  "42544F4E0100000000"},
  {false,                "42544F4E0100000001"},
  {true,                 "42544F4E0100000002"},
  {-32,                  "42544F4E0100000020"},
  {0,                    "42544F4E0100000040"},
  {42,                   "42544F4E010000006A"},
  {95,                   "42544F4E010000009F"},
  {96,                   "42544F4E010000000360000000"},
  {-33,                  "42544F4E0100000003DFFFFFFF"},
  {2_147_483_648,        "42544F4E01000000040000008000000000"},
  {1.5,                  "42544F4E01000000050000C03F"},
  {1.1,                  "42544F4E01000000069A9999999999F13F"},
  {%Raw{bytes: <<1, 2, 3>>}, "42544F4E010000000803000000010203"}
]

Enum.each(vectors, fn {value, expected} ->
  got = encode(value) |> Base.encode16()
  true = got == expected
  # every vector must also decode back to the original value
  true = decode!(encode(value)) == value
  IO.puts("✓ #{inspect(value)} → #{got}")
end)
```

Note the SmallInt boundaries (§26.2): −32, −1, 0, 95 use SmallInt; `96` and
`−33` MUST NOT.

## 4. String table & objects (§10–§11)

Strings are the biggest source of waste in real payloads, so the encoder
collects them in first-encounter order into a per-message table, zero-pads the
table to a multiple of 8, and references them by integer id:

```elixir
encode(%{"age" => 30, "name" => "Alice"}) |> hexdump() |> IO.puts()
```

Reading the bytes: table flag `04`, three entries (`"age"`, `"name"`,
`"Alice"` — keys first, in sorted order, values after), padding `00 00 00 00`,
then the object `0A` with StringRef keys and SmallInt/StringRef values.
Decoders keep the refs lazy and only stringify on access.

## 5. TypedArray and alignment (§13, §16)

A homogeneous numeric array MUST become a TypedArray: element type + count +
padding + raw little-endian buffer, decoded in bulk with no per-element tags.
The padding is what makes zero-copy views possible — the buffer must start at
an address aligned to its element size:

```elixir
encode(%{"score" => 1, "pos" => [1.0, 2.0]}) |> hexdump() |> IO.puts()
```

Walk the offsets: body at 32, object header 5, key ref 2, TypedArray header
7, then `PadLen` = 2 zero bytes — the Float32 buffer starts at offset 48:

```elixir
buf_offset = 8 + 24 + 5 + 2 + 7 + 2  # header + padded table + object hdr + key ref + TA hdr + pad
rem(buf_offset, 4)  # must be 0 for a float32 view
```

The decoder validates the same equation and rejects non-zero padding, so a
malicious or broken encoder cannot smuggle data in padding bytes.

## 6. ObjectTable — column mode (§14)

An array of objects with identical numeric fields becomes a columnar table:
N column reads instead of N×M per-value parses. This is the highest-leverage
path for "100 players" style workloads:

```elixir
table = %ObjectTable{
  columns: [
    {"x", :int8, [1, 3]},
    {"y", :float32, [2.5, 4.5]}
  ]
}

encoded = encode(table)
IO.puts(hexdump(encoded))
{:ok, decoded} = decode(encoded)
to_rows(decoded)
```

The column names go through the string table as StringRefs, each column buffer
is independently aligned to its element size, and `to_rows/1` reconstructs the
original rows by striding across columns.

## 7. Schema mode (§15, §26.6)

When peers share a schema, the encoder omits keys and type tags entirely — the
body is just `SchemaID` + ordered fixed-width values. The schema flag (`0x02`)
signals the body mode, and the embedded schema is authoritative. This is the
exact §26.6 vector:

```elixir
schema = %{
  id: 100,
  name: "Player",
  fields: [{"id", :int32}, {"x", :float32}, {"y", :float32}, {"hp", :uint16}]
}

data = %{"id" => 1, "x" => 1.0, "y" => 2.5, "hp" => 100}

encoded = encode(data, schema: schema)
expected = "42544F4E010200006400000006000000506C617965720400000002000000696404010000007807010000007907020000006870030000000064000000010000000000803F000020406400"

true = Base.encode16(encoded) == expected
true = decode!(encoded) == data
IO.puts("✓ schema vector matches §26.6 byte-for-byte")
decoded = decode!(encoded)
```

No tags, no keys, no string table — the hot path is a struct-cast over the
wire bytes. The 4-byte schema block padding (`00000000`) keeps the body
8-byte aligned.

Flag `0x20` shrinks the schema ID to UInt16. One subtlety the implementation
surfaces: the block saving is **absorbed by the 8-byte padding** (44+4 pad vs.
42+6 pad) — the real saving is the 2-byte `SchemaID` in the body:

```elixir
full = encode(data, schema: schema)
short = encode(data, schema: schema, schema_id_uint16: true)
"block: no change (padding absorbs it), body: 2 bytes saved, total #{byte_size(full) - byte_size(short)}"
```

## 8. Defensive decoding (§19, §24)

The decoder rejects bad magic, unknown tags, trailing bytes, and validates
every padding and ref:

```elixir
{:error, :bad_header} = decode("NOPE" <> encode(1))
{:error, :trailing_bytes} = decode(encode(1) <> <<0>>)
{:error, _} = decode(<<0xFF>>)

:ok
```

Real implementations add configurable limits on nesting depth, string/binary
size, and container counts — a length field can claim gigabytes.

## 9. Scope and limitations

This notebook is a **teaching implementation**, not a production codec:

* no session dictionary — the negotiated dictionary from §11.1/§21.1 (flags
  0x08/0x10) is the piece that makes repeated field names nearly free;
* no extension types (§23) — the tag range `0xF0–0xFF` with the
  `PayloadLength` prefix;
* no change tracking (§20) — an application-level concern;
* strings and binaries are ambiguous in Elixir — use `%Raw{}` for opaque
  bytes, plain binaries for UTF-8 strings;
* decode materializes TypedArrays as lists (the spec allows views where the
  runtime supports zero-copy slices).

For the TOON text side — examples, demos, and Phoenix Channels integration —
see [`toon_ex`](https://hex.pm/packages/toon_ex) on Hex
([docs](https://toon-ex.hexdocs.pm/)) and the spec at
[toonformat.dev](https://toonformat.dev/).
